WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.677
Data types should reflect meaning rather than only the way a value looks.

00:00:03.677 --> 00:00:06.892
A generated identifier can use an identity column.

00:00:06.892 --> 00:00:11.197
Exact monetary values are better represented with an exact numeric type.

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:16.207
Whole-number quantities use integer types.

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:17.677
General descriptive text can use text.

00:00:17.677 --> 00:00:25.202
Birth dates use date, while real-world moments such as appointment times often use timestamp with time zone.

00:00:25.202 --> 00:00:27.622
Boolean is appropriate for genuinely true-or-false states.

00:00:27.622 --> 00:00:31.862
Throughout this course, we also use common PostgreSQL style conventions: uppercase SQL keywords,

00:00:31.862 --> 00:00:38.262
lower snake case for table and column names, and identity columns rather than MySQL-style AUTO_INCREMENT.

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:41.637
Do not choose a type only because a sample value fits.

00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:47.517
Choose the type that represents the meaning and the rules the database should enforce.

00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:52.512
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:59.012
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:01.542
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:01.542 --> 00:01:05.957
In a real setting such as a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing,

00:01:05.957 --> 00:01:12.107
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:14.412
before discussing syntax.

00:01:14.412 --> 00:01:20.834
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
